Boot time is a constant, so answer a constant, and CLOCK_MONOTONIC becomes monotonic.

WHAT WAS WRONG. kern.boottime was recomputed on every call as gettimeofday() minus
sysinfo().uptime, and sysinfo reports uptime in WHOLE SECONDS. The two floors step at different
moments, so the answer alternated between two values ONE SECOND apart. libc derives CLOCK_MONOTONIC
as gettimeofday() minus this, so CLOCK_MONOTONIC WAS NOT MONOTONIC: it returned a smaller value
later in the same thread, and a timed loop measured a NEGATIVE elapsed time, -0.857 s for work that
took 0.156 s. Anything pacing itself off it, animation timing, a timeout, a retry backoff, can see
a second appear or vanish, and a negative interval is the kind of value that makes a loop spin or a
deadline fire immediately.

The old code already knew the value was unstable. It forced tv_usec to 0 with the comment that
jittering microseconds cause an infinite loop in _mach_boottime_usec, which retries until two reads
agree. Zeroing the microseconds stopped the spin and left the whole second jitter.

THE FIX. Compute boot time ONCE from the Linux monotonic clock, which has nanosecond resolution,
and cache it for the life of the process. Boot time IS a constant, so a cache is not an
optimisation, it is the correct answer. Caching is also what makes it safe to return real
microseconds again: a value that never changes cannot spin the retry loop. First writer wins under
a compare and exchange, because two threads computing microseconds apart would otherwise cache two
different constants and put the jitter straight back.

MEASURED, with a control that FAILS BY CONSTRUCTION. tests/foundation/probe_monotonic_clock.c
samples in a tight loop and counts backward steps, and feeds a synthetic sequence with ONE planted
backward step to the same counter first, because a zero from a detector nobody has seen fire is not
evidence.
    CONTROL planted    backward=1 of 4        worst=-500 ns   the detector fires
    CLOCK_MONOTONIC    backward=0 of 299999   worst=0 ns
Three runs, so 0 of 899997 real samples. And in the four clock benchmark the monotonic column now
agrees with wall, mach and cpu to the millisecond on 9 of 9 lines, where it used to be wrong by
exactly 1.000 s on most of them.

Task #251.

